<section image-layout="blank" id="RgewVVHZ0-" background-color="palette-4" card-margin-hidden="true"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="EOPrXjqItB" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">Ozone rescue story</p><h1 font-size="44pt">The Invisible Shield: How Earth Fixed a Hole in the Sky</h1><p font-size="lg">A mystery above Antarctica revealed a global danger, then science and cooperation began repairing it.</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><p font-size="sm">A visual science lesson about ozone, ultraviolet light and a recovery still in progress</p></grid-item><grid-item id="XcQ4xq6ush" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/uGlg2m5h4Uhl4OCVKbAsr.png" saved-media-id="5ct7d9m6o20jow0" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="atmosphere-cut-paper" alt="Layered cut-paper editorial science illustration of Earth curving upward from the lower left, deep ink space above, a thin shield-blue atmospheric arc around the planet, UV-gold sunlight rays meeting the arc with several stopping and one passing through a weakened region, cloud-white ground and subtle action-green signs of repair, clean vector-like edges, subtle paper fibers, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="0cGMAJQNT3"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="CsQQuS7ewq" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><h2>Mystery at the Bottom of the World</h2><p>Each Antarctic spring, Joe Farman, Brian Gardiner and Jon Shanklin saw ozone readings plunge. They checked their instrument. They checked the data. The strange numbers kept returning.</p><p>In May 1985, they reported that ozone above Antarctica was disappearing each spring.</p><aside variant="question"><p><b>Class prediction:</b> If a protective layer high above Earth became thinner, what might reach the ground?</p></aside><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://www.bas.ac.uk/news/marking-40-years-since-the-discovery-of-the-ozone-hole/">British Antarctic Survey</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="qw9bDBUtcM" padding="0" colspan="25"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/nRx7a2lmvdmwNCJlCCwg7.png" saved-media-id="hbkbubskjs5hkl4" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="atmosphere-cut-paper" alt="Layered cut-paper editorial science scene of three Antarctic researchers in heavy field clothing examining a scientific instrument outside a small polar research station, white ice below and deep ink sky above, one researcher comparing a paper chart while the others inspect the machine, shield-blue shadows and restrained UV-gold accents, subtle paper fibers, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="d5NVsDXOD9"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="aOrYsnUHiM"><h2>Earth Wears Sunscreen 15–40 Kilometres Above Us</h2><p>Most ozone sits in the stratosphere, mixed through a wide band of air. It is not a solid roof. Ozone is O₃, three oxygen atoms joined together. The oxygen we breathe is O₂.</p><freeform-block w="840" h="250"><shape preset="ellipse" x="65" y="84" w="710" h="154" fill="none" stroke="palette-1" stroke-width="9"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="105" y="116" w="630" h="128" fill="surface" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="2"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="235" y="52" w="52" h="16" rotation="90" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="400" y="52" w="52" h="16" rotation="90" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="565" y="52" w="52" h="16" rotation="90" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="680" y="74" w="116" h="16" rotation="90" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><box type="content" x="10" y="14" w="180"><p role="eyebrow">Incoming sunlight</p><h4>UVB + UVC</h4></box><box type="content" x="205" y="91" w="430" horizontal-align="center"><labels><label variant="solid">15–40 km</label></labels><h3 align="center">Ozone-rich stratosphere</h3><p align="center">Most UVB and all UVC are absorbed here.</p></box><box type="content" x="585" y="188" w="215"><p align="center"><b>Some UV continues</b><br>toward the surface</p></box><box type="content" x="170" y="189" w="170"><h4 align="center">O₃</h4><p align="center">ozone</p></box><box type="content" x="355" y="189" w="190"><h4 align="center">O₂</h4><p align="center">breathable oxygen</p></box></freeform-block><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://www.epa.gov/ozone-layer-protection/basic-ozone-layer-science">US Environmental Protection Agency</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="6OVWj0CyVd" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="9XeiPD0zFd"><h2>UV Is Invisible, but Its Damage Is Not</h2><p>Ultraviolet radiation carries more energy than visible light. Our eyes cannot see it, but living cells can still absorb its energy.</p><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="Udt9giqRGz" colspan="20"><h4>Invisible rays arrive</h4><p>Extra UVB reaches skin, eyes, crops and tiny ocean organisms.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="cz9H3wBqzD" colspan="20"><h4>Cells take damage</h4><p>Risks include sunburn, skin cancer and cataracts.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="FaCx4pgUS1" colspan="20"><h4>Beads reveal it</h4><p>Cover half of UV-sensitive beads, then compare their color change under a UV torch.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="14pt"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="-z9DaUy2g3" background-color="surface" padding="12pt 18pt"><p><b>Sunscreen protects one person.</b> The ozone layer helps protect life across the planet.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://www.epa.gov/ozone-layer-protection/basic-ozone-layer-science">US Environmental Protection Agency</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="3cuwv0z2up" background-color="palette-4" card-margin-hidden="true"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="veL2b1ESL-" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">The culprit</p><h1>So What Punched the Hole?</h1><p font-size="lg">The answer began inside useful machines near the ground, then drifted far above every city and classroom.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="uqA4uBke7E" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/QFgmM3PWT0bZJkXgbd81T.png" saved-media-id="lod1vbv3wp63o7b" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="atmosphere-cut-paper" alt="Layered cut-paper editorial science illustration of a household refrigerator low in frame releasing a few pale molecule shapes that drift upward in a long hidden path toward a shield-blue atmospheric arc, deep ink upper sky and UV-gold light at the top, scientifically coherent upward journey, clean vector-like edges and subtle paper fibers, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="O_TwbYSz1Z" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="T6HSlJ-Ccr"><h2>A “Safe” Fridge Gas Had a Hidden Journey</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="YVxrQMzvoU" colspan="15"><h4>Useful on Earth</h4><p>CFCs cooled refrigerators and air conditioners and helped make foams and sprays.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="fZFXQiIE81" colspan="15"><h4>Seemingly safe</h4><p>They were stable, nonflammable and not very toxic near the ground.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="sKu92MHA7-" colspan="15"><h4>Slow climb</h4><p>That stability let them drift upward for years without breaking apart.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Dgoo309mh_" colspan="15"><h4>UV unlocks chlorine</h4><p>Powerful sunlight finally split the molecules high in the stratosphere.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="16pt"></spacer><blockquote><p>The problem was not where CFCs were used. It was where they eventually travelled.</p></blockquote><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/how-have-humans-affected-ozone-layer">UNEP Ozone Secretariat</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="6Zlv28Vhuh"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="w30eudwbKz"><h2>One Chlorine Atom Can Wreck Ozone Again and Again</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="XYL_WeS2cn" vertical-align="center" colspan="39"><smart-layout variant="cycle" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/9zf2NyGdT36p69X0HsHz.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="chlorine atom grabbing oxygen"></icon><div><h4>Snatch</h4><p>Chlorine steals one oxygen atom from ozone, leaving O₂.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/YGG04bjHAz4taJYHj8N5.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="oxygen atom released from chlorine"></icon><div><h4>Reset</h4><p>Another reaction makes chlorine let go of that oxygen.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/FJU4auuHQNo32uoq9saFe7.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="repeating robot arm cycle"></icon><div><h4>Repeat</h4><p>The chlorine is free to attack another ozone molecule.</p></div></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></grid-item><grid-item id="PYTFPlga_F" background-color="palette-2" vertical-align="center" colspan="21"><display size="md">100,000</display><h4>ozone molecules</h4><p>One chlorine atom can destroy up to this many before another reaction removes it.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Memory hook:</b> a tiny robot that keeps swinging.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/how-have-humans-affected-ozone-layer">UNEP Ozone Secretariat</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="1DYlpoTLZJ"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="kQuJLGWeEt"><h2>Why Antarctica Gets a “Hole” Every Spring</h2><smart-layout variant="minimalTimeline" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Dark winter</h4><p>The stratosphere becomes extremely cold, and special polar clouds form.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Chlorine is prepared</h4><p>Reactions on the clouds turn stored chlorine into attack-ready chemicals.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Spring sunlight returns</h4><p>Light starts rapid reactions, and ozone falls sharply.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>The size wiggles</h4><p>Changing winds and temperatures make each year different.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="bC97Qsq2oS" background-color="surface" padding="12pt 18pt"><p>The “hole” is not an empty tunnel. It is a huge region where ozone drops below <b>220 Dobson units</b>.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/how-have-humans-affected-ozone-layer">UNEP</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/nasa-noaa-rank-2025-ozone-hole-as-5th-smallest-since-1992/">NASA</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="6-j5JfJsGW"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="A6Sezmb0ku"><h2>The World Made a Planet-Sized Team Decision</h2><p>Scientists found the cause. Countries changed the rules. Engineers redesigned refrigerators, air conditioners, sprays and foams.</p><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="owvfMZSrD8" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">1987</display><h4>Montreal Protocol</h4><p>Countries agreed to stop making and using chemicals that destroy ozone.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="bj3UUzQ3Be" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">Every</display><h4>country joined</h4><p>The treaty eventually became universal.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="zNJC5Xl8ik" background-color="palette-3" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">99%</display><h4>nearly phased out</h4><p>Almost all banned ozone-depleting substances are gone from production and use.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://www.bas.ac.uk/about/history/ozone-hole-discovery/">British Antarctic Survey</a> · <a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/press-release/ozone-layer-recovery-track-helping-avoid-global-warming-05degc">UNEP</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="rcqTTmlRhP"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="lsbevYhhlG"><h2>The Repair Is Working — Slowly</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="ToietNfCAi" background-color="surface" vertical-align="center" colspan="24"><display size="md">18.71 million km²</display><h4>Average 2025 peak-season area</h4><p>The Antarctic hole was still enormous, but it was the fifth smallest since 1992.</p><p>Old CFCs and released chlorine survive for decades, so healing cannot happen overnight.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="uoTEr9Iu1y" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><h3>Expected return to 1980 ozone levels</h3><smart-layout variant="minimalTimeline" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>Around 2040</h4><p>Most of the world</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Around 2045</h4><p>The Arctic</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Around 2066</h4><p>Antarctica</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/nasa-noaa-rank-2025-ozone-hole-as-5th-smallest-since-1992/">NASA</a> · <a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/press-release/ozone-layer-recovery-track-helping-avoid-global-warming-05degc">UNEP</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="JmFNl6dyol"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="52RrxKSRg7"><h2>Scientists Still Have to Watch the Sky</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="rn9nbWyN_K" colspan="25"><labels><label variant="solid">2018 clue</label></labels><p>Measurements showed that CFC-11 had been declining only half as fast after 2012. That pointed to unexpected new emissions.</p><labels><label variant="outline">2019 result</label></labels><p>Observations traced much of the rise to eastern China. Regional emissions later fell back to pre-2013 levels.</p><p><b>The lesson:</b> signing a treaty is only the beginning. Measure, investigate and enforce.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="S_B5Tv4GEq" colspan="35"><h3>Ozone detective kit</h3><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium"><grid-item id="Ircp9ZE0ZU" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/yvFDTCebavfZ1uEEUafC.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="Earth observation satellite" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;The globe's shape formed by a single continuous curved line, divided into sections by wavy lines, symbolizes the earth.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Satellites</h4><p>Scan huge areas from orbit.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="rgZUkKwd-f" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/5lQn9mN7p39p9PvFSlYy.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="weather balloon instrument package" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A cloud shape with falling lines underneath, symbolizing rain or precipitation, conveying the concept of weather, specifically related to rain or stormy conditions.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Weather balloons</h4><p>Carry sensors through the atmosphere.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="3c0kLO8WBI" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/7QyUdiKfghs6DQuXC3Wc4d.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="atmospheric ground monitoring station" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A basic symbol consisting of a short vertical line intersecting with a longer horizontal line at its midpoint, signifying a stopping or waiting area, often associated with transportation, travel, or a designated point of arrival or departure.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Ground stations</h4><p>Build long records from one place.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="diV6XqLMgf" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/uTVLGH76Oo4k6Gy6NUQh.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="sealed laboratory air sample flask" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black petri dish with a curved lid, symbolizing the concept of a laboratory where scientific experiments and research are conducted.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Air samples</h4><p>Reveal tiny amounts of invisible gas.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0106-2">Nature, 2018</a> · <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03277-w">Nature, 2021</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="5RY-Nx1Hsk" vertical-align="center" background-color="accent"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="-E-_t5U4dQ" vertical-align="center" colspan="18"><display size="lg">03</display></grid-item><grid-item id="pIqhu_qbWg" vertical-align="center" colspan="42"><p role="eyebrow">The comeback continues</p><h1>Your Mission: Protect the Comeback</h1><p font-size="lg">Keep cooling gases contained, make responsible choices and stay UV-smart while the atmosphere heals.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="R2A0td5MDv" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="_gOB1bRWl6"><h2>Three Smart Moves Beat a Hundred Tiny Gestures</h2><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-straddle="true" spacing="medium" padding-top="44pt"><grid-item id="_Bx1CEDYts" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/4SeWnp7iH3SpCokLehRT.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="technician recovering refrigerator refrigerant" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black rectangular box with a flat top and a handle on the front, symbolizing a common household appliance used for keeping food and drinks cold.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Contain cooling gases</h3><p>Use trained technicians. Dispose of old fridges and air conditioners properly so refrigerants are recovered, not released.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="3kSltc2Ora" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/lUDa8b1HZo9XxTJF6jlt.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="responsible cooling equipment choice" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Two crossed industrial tools, forming the concept of equipment or machinery.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Ask better questions</h3><p>When adults buy cooling equipment or insulation, ask about ozone-safe, climate-friendly choices.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="tFoLFaQ78v" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/HqRnqTOMvbrtWfZHXPTo.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="sun protection hat shade sunscreen" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Sun symbolized by a circular shape with rays extending outwards, embodies the concept of the sun, conveying warmth, light, and energy.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Protect yourself now</h3><p>Check the UV Index. Seek shade, wear protective clothing and use sunscreen while healing continues.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="14pt"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="A-Nyb36k9l" background-color="palette-3" padding="12pt 18pt"><p><b>Pass on the rescue chain:</b> science found the problem, cooperation changed products, and patient monitoring is repairing the shield.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Y-aGGGJuJY"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="gvMpvJ67N0"><h2>Final Challenge: Build the Ozone Rescue Chain</h2><p>In teams, arrange six cards into the true cause-and-effect order.</p><grid marker="number" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="small"><grid-item id="HszSGLryZL" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>CFCs escape</h4><p>Cooling and spray products release them.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="POsdWeFSkC" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>CFCs drift upward</h4><p>Stable molecules reach the stratosphere.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="9UvxGJXKcg" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>UV releases chlorine</h4><p>Powerful sunlight splits the CFCs.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="IsoMqiSol-" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Chlorine repeats</h4><p>One atom attacks ozone again and again.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="94IsC8Hpyt" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>More UV reaches Earth</h4><p>The weakened layer absorbs less.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="QVQnTix9DN" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Countries act</h4><p>CFCs are phased out, and ozone slowly heals.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="12pt"></spacer><aside variant="question"><p>Remove one card and explain what breaks. Then finish: <b>“The ozone story proves that…”</b></p></aside></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="IY6pAiD-5K" background-color="palette-4" card-margin-hidden="true"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="c-tiqmVZVG" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">The lesson to keep</p><h1 font-size="36pt">A Hole in the Sky Became Proof We Can Repair Harm</h1><p><b>Remember the shield:</b> ozone high in the stratosphere blocks dangerous UV.</p><p><b>Remember the chain reaction:</b> chlorine from CFCs can destroy ozone repeatedly.</p><p><b>Remember the comeback:</b> the world changed its rules, nearly eliminated the worst chemicals and put recovery on track.</p><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="vKNVuIjoWv" background-color="palette-3" padding="12pt 16pt"><p><b>Exit ticket:</b> Draw four panels: shield → damage → teamwork → healing.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="jmgrO-fnmR" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/pFjdehzyAodNECs5zFP9I.png" saved-media-id="8qa91j7qrsgxu4b" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="atmosphere-cut-paper" alt="Layered cut-paper editorial science illustration matching the opening Earth orientation, Earth curving upward from the lower left beneath deep ink space, a thin shield-blue atmospheric arc now continuous and strengthened with a restrained action-green recovery seam, UV-gold rays meeting the arc with most visibly stopping, cloud-white ground, clean vector-like edges and subtle paper fibers, hopeful and scientifically coherent, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>